  <p>High-income economies tax fuel at an average rate of <strong>""" + f"{s['tax_high_income']:.1f}" + """%</strong>, compared to """ + f"{s['tax_low_income']:.1f}" + """% in low-income economies. This tax differential is the primary driver of the 2.6× price gap between high- and low-income countries. The tax revenue funds infrastructure and environmental programs in high-income nations, while in low-income countries, lower taxation reflects both limited fiscal capacity and the political sensitivity of fuel costs.</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 6: KEY FINDINGS & POLICY IMPLICATIONS ═══════════ -->
<section class="section" id="key-findings-recommendations">
  <h2>6. Key Findings &amp; Policy Implications</h2>
  <p class="objective">Synthesize the most important insights from the analysis into actionable findings, including recommendations for policymakers on subsidy design, tax calibration, and price stabilization mechanisms.</p>

  <ol class="finding-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Brent Crude is the dominant price driver across all markets.</strong> The near-perfect Pearson correlation (r = """ + f"{s['corr_petrol_brent']:.4f}" + """) between Brent Crude and average retail petrol prices confirms that global benchmark prices dictate retail fuel costs regardless of region, income level, or subsidy regime. Policymakers should treat crude oil price forecasting as the single most important input to fuel price projections.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Subsidies reduce price levels but do not stabilize prices.</strong> High-subsidy countries pay ~""" + f"{s['dampening_petrol_pct']:.0f}" + """% less at the pump than low-subsidy countries ($""" + f"{s['high_subsidy_avg_petrol']:.3f}" + """ vs. $""" + f"{s['low_subsidy_avg_petrol']:.3f}" + """/L), yet both groups exhibit identical Brent-to-retail correlation. Subsidies are a blunt instrument for consumer protection — they lower the baseline but leave households fully exposed to global price swings. To achieve price <em>stability</em>, governments should complement subsidies with stabilization funds, strategic petroleum reserves, or price-smoothing mechanisms.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The regional price gap is structural and persistent.</strong> European consumers pay $""" + f"{s['highest_region_petrol']:.3f}" + """/L while Middle Eastern consumers pay $""" + f"{s['lowest_region_petrol']:.3f}" + """/L — a """ + f"{s['highest_region_petrol']/s['lowest_region_petrol']:.1f}" + """× difference driven overwhelmingly by taxation policy. The regional spread has not narrowed over the six-year period, indicating deeply embedded structural differences rather than cyclical phenomena.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>High-income economies impose significantly higher fuel taxes.</strong> The average tax rate of """ + f"{s['tax_high_income']:.1f}" + """% in high-income countries is """ + f"{s['tax_high_income']/s['tax_low_income']:.1f}" + """× the """ + f"{s['tax_low_income']:.1f}" + """% rate in low-income countries. This tax gap serves dual purposes in developed economies: revenue generation for infrastructure and a de facto carbon price signal. Developing economies that keep taxes low to protect household budgets forgo both fiscal revenue and environmental incentives.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The COVID-19 demand shock created the most extreme price dislocation of the period.</strong> Weekly petrol prices fell by −6.85% and −6.84% in consecutive weeks in March 2020 — the largest declines in the dataset. Yet the recovery was equally dramatic: average petrol prices surged """ + f"{s['petrol_2020_to_2022']:.1f}" + """% from 2020 to 2022. The speed and magnitude of these swings highlight the critical importance of maintaining fiscal buffers and flexible subsidy mechanisms that can respond to extreme market conditions.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Energy affordability is a multi-dimensional challenge.</strong> While low-income countries have lower absolute pump prices ($""" + f"{s['petrol_trough_week_value']:.3f}" + """ to $3.547/L range for different income segments), their consumers face higher price volatility relative to income. The coefficient of variation is highest in low-income countries (""" + f"{s['cv_low_income']:.1f}" + """%), meaning a given price swing consumes a larger share of household budgets. Policymakers in these markets should prioritize targeted cash transfers or tiered pricing over broad-based subsidies.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Policy recommendation:</strong> The evidence strongly suggests that governments should (1) decouple consumer protection objectives from blanket fuel subsidies — which are regressive, fiscally costly, and ineffective at price stabilization; (2) adopt price-smoothing mechanisms (e.g., automatic excise tax adjustments that move inversely with crude prices); and (3) redirect subsidy savings toward targeted direct transfers to vulnerable households. This approach preserves the price signal for efficient consumption while protecting those most affected by fuel price volatility.
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ DATA NOTE ═══════════ -->
<section class="section">
  <h2>Data Notes &amp; Limitations</h2>
  <ul style="padding-left:20px; color:#555; font-size:0.9rem;">
    <li>Dataset: 27,468 weekly observations across 84 countries, January 2020 – April 2026. Source: Kaggle (belbino/global-fuel-prices-20202026), aggregated from EIA, OPEC, World Bank, and national energy ministries.</li>
    <li>All prices normalized to USD/liter using historical spot exchange rates. Brent Crude is the global benchmark in USD/barrel.</li>
    <li>Tax percentage shows significant week-to-week variability within countries (ranging from ~15% to ~65%), which may reflect estimation methodology or compositional changes rather than actual policy shifts. Analysts should treat individual-week tax figures as indicative rather than precise.</li>
    <li>Subsidy level is observed with four categories in the data (Very High, High, Medium, Low) rather than the two documented in the schema. All four are reported in this analysis.</li>
    <li>Extreme values at the lower bound ($0.010/L) reflect heavily subsidized markets with administered pricing regimes and should be interpreted in that context.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

