  <div class="callout callout-warn">
    <strong>Critical finding:</strong> While high subsidies dramatically reduce the <em>absolute price level</em> that consumers pay (by ~{s['dampening_petrol_pct']:.0f}%), they do <strong>not</strong> meaningfully reduce the <em>correlation</em> between Brent Crude movements and retail price changes. Both high- and low-subsidy regimes exhibit near-perfect Brent-to-retail correlation (r &asymp; 1.0). This means subsidies function as a <strong>level shifter</strong> (lowering baseline prices) rather than a <strong>stabilizer</strong> (reducing volatility). Governments seeking price stability must look beyond fuel subsidies to mechanisms like price ceilings, strategic petroleum reserves, or automatic tax adjustments that move inversely with crude prices.
