    <strong>Energy equity paradox:</strong> High-income countries pay the highest absolute fuel prices (<strong>${s['petrol_2025_avg']:.3f}/L</strong> avg. petrol) but face <strong>lower relative volatility</strong> (coefficient of variation: {s['cv_high_income']:.1f}%). Conversely, low-income countries pay lower absolute prices but with higher relative volatility (CV: {s['cv_low_income']:.1f}%), creating acute budgeting challenges for households with already-constrained resources. A given crude oil price shock translates into a proportionally larger income shock in developing economies.
