l-trends">", "   <h2>2. Temporal Price Trends &amp; Volatility</h2>", "   <p class="objective">Analyze weekly and yearly price trends for each fuel type, identify periods of peak volatility, and quantify the correlation between Brent Crude movements and retail price changes.</p>", " ", "-  <p>The global retail fuel market between 2020 and 2026 was shaped by three distinct regimes: the <strong>COVID-19 demand collapse</strong> (Q1–Q2 2020), the <strong>post-pandemic recovery and 2022 energy crisis</strong>, and a <strong>prolonged high-plateau period</strong> (2023–2026) where Brent Crude averaged above $120/bbl. Retail prices closely tracked these swings, with the Pearson correlation between Brent and average retail petrol reaching <strong>r = """ + f"{s['corr_petrol_brent']:.4f}" + """</strong>.</p>", "+  <p>The global retail fuel market between 2020 and 2026 was shaped by three distinct regimes: the <strong>COVID-19 demand collapse</strong> (Q1–Q2 2020), the <strong>post-pandemic recovery and 2022 energy crisis</strong>, and a <strong>prolonged high-plateau period</strong> (2023–2026) during which Brent Crude consistently averaged above $120/bbl. Retail prices tracked these swings with remarkable fidelity: the Pearson correlation between Brent and aggregate retail petrol reached <strong>r = {s['corr_petrol_brent']:.4f}</strong>, indicating an essentially deterministic relationship at the aggregate level.</p>", " ", "+  <div class="callout callout-warn">", "+    <strong>Volatility analysis:</strong> The largest weekly petrol price declines occurred during the <strong>COVID-19 crash of March–April 2020</strong>, with week-over-week drops of −6.85% (16 Mar 2020) and −6.84% (9 Mar 2020) as Brent Crude plummeted from $71 to below $48/bbl. The recovery was equally sharp: average annual petrol prices surged <strong>+{s['petrol_2020_to_2022']:.1f}%</strong> from 2020 to 2022 ($<strong>{s['petrol_2020_avg']:.3f}/L</strong> to $<strong>{s['petrol_2022_avg']:.3f}/L</strong>)