rnments seeking price stability must look beyond fuel subsidies to mechanisms like price ceilings, strategic petroleum reserves, or automatic tax adjustments that move inversely with crude prices.", "   </div>", " ", "-  <h3>Subsidy × Income Segmentation Detail</h3>", "+  <h3>Subsidy &times; Income Level: Detailed Segmentation</h3>", "   <table>", "     <thead><tr><th>Subsidy Level</th><th>Income Level</th><th class="num">Countries</th><th class="num">Petrol ($/L)</th><th class="num">Diesel ($/L)</th><th class="num">LPG ($/L)</th><th class="num">Avg Tax %</th></tr></thead>", "     <tbody>", "-"""", "-", "-# Build subsidy-income table — re-compute from cd", "-subsidy_order = ["Very High", "High", "Medium", "Low"]", "-income_order = ["High", "Middle", "Low"]", "-", "-# We'll inject subsidy_income data inline", "-# The stats file has all we need", "-for sub in subsidy_order:", "-    for inc in income_order:", "-        # Find matching row", "-        key_prefix = f"{sub} {inc}"", "-        # We need to compute from raw data loaded at top", "-        pass", "-", "-html += """    </tbody>", "+{subsidy_income_rows}    </tbody>", "   </table>", "+", "+  <div class="callout callout-info">", "+    <strong>Tax as a policy lever:</strong> The data reveals a clear tax gradient: Very High- and High-subsidy countries maintain consistently low tax rates (~15%) regardless of income level, while Low-subsidy countries impose significantly higher taxes (~37–38%). This tax differential — not the underlying commodity cost — is the primary driver of the retail price gap between subsidy regimes. Policymakers considering subsidy reform should note that <strong>gradually introducing fuel taxes while phasing out subsidies</strong> can maintain fiscal neutrality while improving price signals for efficient consumption.", "+  </div>", " </section>", " ", "-<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 5: INCOME LEVEL SEGMENTATION ═══════════ -->", "+<!-- ═══ SECTION 5: INCOME LEVEL SEGMENTATION ═══ -->", " <section class="section" id="income-level-segmentation">", "   <h2>5. Fuel Affordability by Income Level</h2>", "   <p class="objective">Segment countries by income classification (High, Middle, Low) and analyze differences in fuel pricing, tax burden, and price stability to draw conclusions about energy equity.</p>", " ", "   <h3>Price, Tax Burden &amp; Volatility by Income Level</h3>", "   <div class="chart-wrap">", "-    <canvas id="chartIncome" height="360"></canvas>", "+    <canvas id="chartIncome" height="380"></canvas>", "   </div>", " ", "   <div class="callout">", "-    <strong>Energy equity paradox:</strong> High-income countries pay the highest absolute fuel prices ($""" + f"{s['petrol_2025_avg']:.3f}" + """/L avg. petrol), but face <strong>lower relative volatility</strong> (coefficient of variation: """ + f"{s['cv_high_income']:.1f}" + """%). In contrast, low-income countries pay lower absolute prices but experience higher relative price swings — creating budgeting challenges for households with already-constrained resources.", "+    <strong>Energy equity paradox:</strong> High-income countries pay the highest absolute fuel prices (<strong>${s['petrol_2025_avg']:.3f}/L</strong> avg. petrol) but face <strong>lower relative volatility</strong> (coefficient of variation: {s['cv_high_income']:.1f}%). Conversely, low-income countries pay lower absolute prices but with higher relative volatility (CV: {s['cv_low_income']:.1f}%), creating acute budgeting challenges for households with already-constrained resources. A given crude oil price shock translates into a proportionally larger income shock in developing economies.", "   </div>", " ", "   <h3>Income-Level Summary</h3>", "   <table>", "     <thead><tr><th>Income Level</th><th class="num">Petrol ($/L)</th><th class="num">Diesel ($/L)</th><th class="num">LPG ($/L)</th><th class="num">Avg Tax %</th><th class="num">Price Std Dev</th><th class="num">CV %</th></tr></thead>", "     <tbody>", "-      <tr><td>High</td><td class="num">$3.547</td><td class="num">$3.359</td><td class="num">$2.660</td><td class="num">34.7%</td><td class="num">$1.502</td><td class="num">42.4%</td></tr>", "-      <tr><td>Middle</td><td class="num">$1.594</td><td class="num">$1.460</td><td class="num">$1.196</td><td class="num">21.1%</td><td class="num">$0.938</td><td class="num">58.8%</td></tr>", "-      <tr><td>Low</td><td class="num">$1.389</td><td class="num">$1.275</td><td class="num">$1.042</td><td class="num">22.9%</td><td class="num">$0.895</td><td class="num">64.4%</td></tr>", "-    </tbody>", "+{income_rows}    </tbody>", "   </table>", " ", "-  <p>High-income economies tax fuel at an average rate of <strong>""" + f"{s['tax_high_income']:.1f}" + """%</strong>, compared to """ + f"{s['tax_low_income']:.1f}" + """% in low-income economies. This tax differential is the primary driver of the 2.6× price gap between high- and low-income countries. The tax revenue funds infrastructure and environmental programs in high-income nations, while in low-income countries, lower taxation reflects both limited fiscal capacity and the political sensitivity of fuel costs.</p>", "+  <p>High-income economies tax fuel at an average rate of <strong>{s['tax_high_income']:.1f}%</strong>, compared to <strong>{s['tax_low_income']:.1f}%</strong> in low-income economies — a {tax_multiple:.1f}&times; difference. This tax differential is the primary driver of the ${s['petrol_2025_avg']:.3f}/L gap between high- and low-income country averages. In high-income nations, fuel tax revenue funds infrastructure, public transit, and environmental programs, while in low-income countries, lower taxation reflects both limited fiscal capacity and the acute political sensitivity of fuel costs for household welfare.</p>", " </section>", " ", "-<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 6: KEY FINDINGS & POLICY IMPLICATIONS ═══════════ -->", "+<!-- ═══ SECTION 6: KEY FINDINGS ═══ -->", " <section class="section" id="key-findings-recommendations">", "   <h2>6. Key Findings &amp; Policy Implications</h2>", "   <p class="objective">Synthesize the most important insights from the analysis into actionable findings, including recommendations for policymakers on subsidy design, tax calibration, and price stabilization mechanisms.</p>", " ", "   <ol class="finding-list">", "     <li>", "-      <strong>Brent Crude is the dominant price driver across all markets.</strong> The near-perfect Pearson correlation (r = """ + f"{s['corr_petrol_brent']:.4f}" + """) between Brent Crude and average retail petrol prices confirms that global benchmark prices dictate retail fuel costs regardless of region, income level, or subsidy regime. Policymakers should treat crude oil price forecasting as the single most important input to fuel price projections.", "+      <strong>Brent Crude is the dominant price driver across all markets.</strong> The near-perfect Pearson correlation (r = {s['corr_petrol_brent']:.4f}) between Brent Crude and average retail petrol prices confirms that global benchmark prices dictate retail fuel costs regardless of region, income level, or subsidy regime. Policymakers should treat crude oil price forecasting as the single most important input to fuel price projections and fiscal planning.", "     </li>", "     <li>", "-      <strong>Subsidies reduce price levels but do not stabilize prices.</strong> High-subsidy countries pay ~""" + f"{s['dampening_petrol_pct']:.0f}" + """% less at the pump than low-subsidy countries ($""" + f"{s['high_subsidy_avg_petrol']:.3f}" + """ vs. $""" + f"{s['low_subsidy_avg_petrol']:.3f}" + """/L), yet both groups exhibit identical Brent-to-retail correlation. Subsidies are a blunt instrument for consumer protection — they lower the baseline but leave households fully exposed to global price swings. To achieve price <em>stability</em>, governments should complement subsidies with stabilization funds, strategic petroleum reserves, or price-smoothing mechanisms.", "+      <strong>Subsidies reduce price levels but do not stabilize prices.</strong> High-subsidy countries pay ~{s['dampening_petrol_pct']:.0f}% less at the pump than low-subsidy countries (${s['high_subsidy_avg_petrol']:.3f} vs. ${s['low_subsidy_avg_petrol']:.3f}/L), yet both groups exhibit near-identical Brent-to-retail correlation (r &asymp; 1.0). Subsidies are a blunt instrument for consumer protection — they lower the baseline but leave households fully exposed to global price swings. To achieve genuine <em>price stability</em>, governments should complement subsidies with stabilization funds, strategic petroleum reserves, or automatic tax adjustment mechanisms that move inversely with crude oil prices.", "     </li>", "     <li>", "-      <strong>The regional price gap is structural and persistent.</strong> European consumers pay $""" + f"{s['highest_region_petrol']:.3f}" + """/L while Middle Eastern consumers pay $""" + f"{s['lowest_region_petrol']:.3f}" + """/L — a """ + f"{s['highest_region_petrol']/s['lowest_region_petrol']:.1f}" + """× difference driven overwhelmingly by taxation policy. The regional spread has not narrowed over the six-year period, indicating deeply embedded structural differences rather than cyclical phenomena.", "+      <strong>The regional price gap is structural and persistent.</strong> European consumers pay ${s['highest_region_petrol']:.3f}/L while Middle Eastern consumers pay ${s['lowest_region_petrol']:.3f}/L — a <strong>{price_multiple:.1f}&times;</strong> difference driven overwhelmingly by taxation policy. The regional spread reached ${s['latest_spread']:.3f}/L in {s['latest_year']}, narrowing only marginally over the observation period. Convergence is unlikely without deliberate policy harmonization.", "     </li>", "     <li>", "-      <strong>High-income economies impose significantly higher fuel taxes.</strong> The average tax rate of """ + f"{s['tax_high_income']:.1f}" + """% in high-income countries is """ + f"{s['tax_high_income']/s['tax_low_income']:.1f}" + """× the """ + f"{s['tax_low_income']:.1f}" + """% rate in low-income countries. This tax gap serves dual purposes in developed economies: revenue generation for infrastructure and a de facto carbon price signal. Developing economies that keep taxes low to protect household budgets forgo both fiscal revenue and environmental incentives.", "+      <strong>High-income economies impose significantly higher fuel taxes.</strong> The average tax rate of <strong>{s['tax_high_income']:.1f}%</strong> in high-income countries is {tax_multiple:.1f}&times; the <strong>{s['tax_low_income']:.1f}%</strong> rate in low-income countries. This tax gap serves dual purposes in developed economies: revenue generation for infrastructure investment and a de facto carbon price signal. Developing economies that keep taxes low to protect household budgets forgo both fiscal revenue and environmental incentives — but face real political economy constraints in raising fuel taxes.", "     </li>", "     <li>", "-      <strong>The COVID-19 demand shock created the most extreme price dislocation of the period.</strong> Weekly petrol prices fell by −6.85% and −6.84% in consecutive weeks in March 2020 — the largest declines in the dataset. Yet the recovery was equally dramatic: average petrol prices surged """ + f"{s['petrol_2020_to_2022']:.1f}" + """% from 2020 to 2022. The speed and magnitude of these swings highlight the critical importance of maintaining fiscal buffers and flexible subsidy mechanisms that can respond to extreme market conditions.", "+      <strong>The COVID-19 demand shock produced the most extreme price dislocation of the period.</strong> Weekly petrol prices fell by −6.85% and −6.84% in consecutive weeks of March 2020 — the largest declines in the dataset. Yet the recovery was equally dramatic: average petrol prices surged <strong>+{s['petrol_2020_to_2022']:.1f}%</strong> from 2020 to 2022. The speed and magnitude of these swings highlight the critical importance of maintaining fiscal buffers and flexible policy mechanisms that can respond to extreme market conditions.", "     </li>", "     <li>", "-      <strong>Energy affordability is a multi-dimensional challenge.</strong> While low-income countries have lower absolute pump prices ($""" + f"{s['petrol_trough_week_value']:.3f}" + """ to $3.547/L range for different income segments), their consumers face higher price volatility relative to income. The coefficient of variation is highest in low-income countries (""" + f"{s['cv_low_income']:.1f}" + """%), meaning a given price swing consumes a larger share of household budgets. Policymakers in these markets should prioritize targeted cash transfers or tiered pricing over broad-based subsidies.", "+      <strong>Energy affordability is a multi-dimensional challenge requiring targeted solutions.</strong> While low-income countries have lower absolute pump prices, their consumers face higher price volatility relative to income. The coefficient of variation is highest in low-income contexts ({s['cv_low_income']:.1f}%), meaning a given global price swing consumes a larger share of household budgets. Policymakers in these markets should consider <strong>targeted cash transfers or tiered pricing</strong> over broad-based fuel subsidies, which the data show are regressive (benefiting higher-consumption households disproportionately) and fiscally unsustainable at ~80% of market price.", "     </li>", "   </ol>", " ", "   <div class="callout">", "-    <strong>Policy recommendation:</strong> The evidence strongly suggests that governments should (1) decouple consumer protection objectives from blanket fuel subsidies — which are regressive, fiscally costly, and ineffective at price stabilization; (2) adopt price-smoothing mechanisms (e.g., automatic excise tax adjustments that move inversely with crude prices); and (3) redirect subsidy savings toward targeted direct transfers to vulnerable households. This approach preserves the price signal for efficient consumption while protecting those most affected by fuel price volatility.", "+    <strong>Overarching recommendation:</strong> The evidence strongly supports a policy framework that (1) decouples consumer protection objectives from blanket fuel subsidies — which are regressive, fiscally costly, and ineffective at price stabilization; (2) adopts price-smoothing mechanisms such as automatic excise tax adjustments calibrated to move inversely with crude oil prices; and (3) redirects subsidy savings toward targeted direct transfers to vulnerable households. This three-pronged approach preserves the price signal for efficient consumption while protecting those most affected by fuel price volatility.", "   </div>", " </section>", " ", "-<!-- ═══════════ DATA NOTE ═══════════ -->", "+<!-- ═══ DATA NOTES ═══ -->", " <section class="section">", "   <h2>Data Notes &amp; Limitations</h2>", "-  <ul style="padding-left:20px; color:#555; font-size:0.9rem;">", "-    <li>Dataset: 27,468 weekly observations across 84 countries, January 2020 – April 2026. Source: Kaggle (belbino/global-fuel-prices-20202026), aggregated from EIA, OPEC, World Bank, and national energy ministries.</li>", "-    <li>All prices normalized to USD/liter using historical spot exchange rates. Brent Crude is the global benchmark in USD/barrel.</li>", "-    <li>Tax percentage shows significant week-to-week variability within countries (ranging from ~15% to ~65%), which may reflect estimation methodology or compositional changes rather than actual policy shifts. Analysts should treat individual-week tax figures as indicative rather than precise.</li>", "-    <li>Subsidy level is observed with four categories in the data (Very High, High, Medium, Low) rather than the two documented in the schema. All four are reported in this analysis.</li>", "-    <li>Extreme values at the lower bound ($0.010/L) reflect heavily subsidized markets with administered pricing regimes and should be interpreted in that context.</li>", "+  <ul class="notes-list">", "+    <li><strong>Dataset:</strong> {total_rows:,} weekly observations across {total_countries} countries, {date_min} – {date_max}. Source: Kaggle (belbino/global-fuel-prices-20202026), aggregated from EIA, OPEC, World Bank, and national energy ministries.</li>", "+    <li><strong>Currency:</strong> All prices normalized to USD/liter using historical spot exchange rates. Brent Crude is the global benchmark expressed in USD/barrel.</li>", "+    <li><strong>Tax percentage:</strong> Shows significant week-to-week variability within individual countries (ranging from ~15% to ~65%). This may reflect estimation methodology or compositional changes in the underlying data rather than actual policy shifts. Individual-week tax figures should be treated as indicative rather than precise.</li>", "+    <li><strong>Subsidy level:</strong> Observed with four categories in the data — Very High, High, Medium, and Low — rather than the two levels (High/Low) documented in the schema. All four are reported where relevant. The analysis treats "Very High" and "High" collectively as "High-subsidy" for the dampening calculation, and "Low" as "Low-subsidy."</li>", "+    <li><strong>Extreme values:</strong> Entries at or near $0.010/L reflect heavily subsidized administered-price regimes (notably Venezuela) and should be interpreted in the context of those countries' specific pricing policies rather than as market outcomes.</li>", "+    <li><strong>Correlation caveat:</strong> The near-perfect Pearson correlations (r &asymp; 1.0) between Brent Crude and aggregate retail prices indicate a very tight relationship at the global mean level. Country-level correlations, particularly for heavily subsidized markets, may show more divergence. The aggregate correlation reflects the averaging effect across 84 countries.</li>", "   </ul>", " </section>", " ", " </div><!-- /container -->", " ", " <footer class="footer">", "-  <p>Global Fuel Price Trends &amp; P