  <p>The near-unity correlation across all three fuel types indicates that <strong>global crude oil benchmarks are the overwhelmingly dominant driver of retail fuel prices</strong> at the aggregate level, explaining essentially all observed variation. This tight coupling constrains the degree to which national policies can decouple retail prices from international markets — though the absolute price <em>level</em> still varies dramatically by country due to taxation and subsidy interventions.</p>
